00:49:33,735 --> 00:49:35,765
I just pushed the button.

1498
00:49:35,865 --> 00:49:37,164
- How many stops are left?
- I'm serious.

1499
00:49:37,165 --> 00:49:39,634
- Are you telling the truth?
- Yes. We're really almost there.

1500
00:49:39,635 --> 00:49:41,774
- I'm starting to...
- Tell us in minutes.

1501
00:49:41,775 --> 00:49:43,774
- In minutes? Hold on.
- Is it going to take ten minutes?

1502
00:49:43,775 --> 00:49:44,874
- Where are we now?
- I can't stand it anymore.

1503
00:49:44,875 --> 00:49:46,074
Tell us if it'll take 30 minutes.

1504
00:49:46,075 --> 00:49:47,275
- It's...
- I've had enough, Sun Bin.

1505
00:49:47,975 --> 00:49:49,784
- Yes. We'll be there in 2 minutes.
- In 2 minutes?

1506
00:49:49,785 --> 00:49:51,014
The terminal will be there after we make a turn here.

1507
00:49:51,015 --> 00:49:52,515
- Okay.
- Yes. I'm serious.

1508
00:49:52,655 --> 00:49:55,084
This is when we say, "We're almost there" in Seoul.

1509
00:49:55,085 --> 00:49:58,025
- This is about it.
- We said that 30 minutes ago.

1510
00:49:58,755 --> 00:50:01,454
- It's been exactly 32 minutes.
- Come on, Jae Myung.

1511
00:50:01,455 --> 00:50:03,224
We're getting off now. We should get ready.

1512
00:50:03,225 --> 00:50:05,035
- Sure.
- For real?

1513
00:50:05,135 --> 00:50:06,464
- Yes. Get ready.
- It's the last stop.

1514
00:50:06,465 --> 00:50:07,795
- For real.
- Everyone will get off.

1515
00:50:08,965 --> 00:50:11,305
- Let's get off.
- Let's get off. I'm serious.

1516
00:50:11,905 --> 00:50:13,075
Which way?

1517
00:50:14,075 --> 00:50:16,805
- Many stores have disappeared.
- I remember filming downtown.

1518
00:50:17,005 --> 00:50:19,815
- The department store is huge.
- Yes.

1519
00:50:19,975 --> 00:50:21,684
Look at that sculpture.

1520
00:50:21,685 --> 00:50:23,745
- Look at the red bag.
- What is it?

1521
00:50:24,645 --> 00:50:26,884
- That's a landmark in Cheonan.
- Goodness.

1522
00:50:26,885 --> 00:50:28,054
Why is that a landmark?

1523
00:50:28,055 --> 00:50:30,054
- Is that a meeting point?
- That's right.

1524
00:50:30,055 --> 00:50:31,325
"Hey, come to the red bag."

1525
00:50:32,425 --> 00:50:33,694
- Exactly.
- That's it.

1526
00:50:33,695 --> 00:50:35,224
- There's always a meeting point.
- In every city.

1527
00:50:35,225 --> 00:50:36,294
- I see.
- Of course.

1528
00:50:36,295 --> 00:50:37,695
Wait. Hold on. Look.

1529
00:50:38,435 --> 00:50:39,495
The walnut cakes.

1530
00:50:39,665 --> 00:50:40,694
There are many walnut cake shops...

1531
00:50:40,695 --> 00:50:41,704
- on our way.
- Near here?

1532
00:50:41,705 --> 00:50:42,864
That's the main branch.

1533
00:50:42,865 --> 00:50:45,734
- Is walnut cake all he sees?
- Jae Seok.

1534
00:50:45,735 --> 00:50:48,175
Let's go buy some after lunch.

1535
00:50:48,575 --> 00:50:50,645
- You do love walnut cakes.
- Seriously. Yes.

1536
00:50:51,615 --> 00:50:53,945
We have to buy some.

1537
00:50:54,245 --> 00:50:55,245
They're not on break, are they?

1538
00:50:55,246 --> 00:50:57,514
- Gosh. Hello.
- Hello.

1539
00:50:57,515 --> 00:50:58,985
- Hello.
- Should we sit here?

1540
00:50:59,855 --> 00:51:01,554
- Do we just sit like this?
- Yes.

1541
00:51:01,555 --> 00:51:02,825
- This is interesting.
- Why?

1542
00:51:03,585 --> 00:51:04,824
(The variety-show rookie finds everything interesting.)

1543
00:51:04,825 --> 00:51:06,025
It's good.

1544
00:51:06,925 --> 00:51:08,794
- There's only one dish on the menu.
- What?

1545
00:51:08,795 --> 00:51:10,164
- Is there only one dish?
- Mulmakguksu.

1546
00:51:10,165 --> 00:51:11,525
Don't they have mulmakguksu?

1547
00:51:11,665 --> 00:51:13,095
- Do they only sell one dish?
- Yes.

1548
00:51:13,535 --> 00:51:14,634
There's only one dish.

1549
00:51:14,635 --> 00:51:16,934
- We'll get four.
- Yes.

1550
00:51:16,935 --> 00:51:18,805
- You don't serve broth, do you?
- No.

1551
00:51:19,535 --> 00:51:20,735
That's the only menu item.

1552
00:51:21,075 --> 00:51:23,504
We have to play a game to pay for lunch.

1553
00:51:23,505 --> 00:51:24,505
- Yes.
- That's right.

1554
00:51:24,506 --> 00:51:26,215
We're only having makguksu, though.

1555
00:51:26,915 --> 00:51:28,115
I have an idea, Jae Seok.

1556
00:51:29,145 --> 00:51:31,084
Should we include walnut cakes too?

1557
00:51:31,085 --> 00:51:32,354
(Should we add walnut cakes to the bet?)

1558
00:51:32,355 --> 00:51:35,025
- At once?
- You're obsessed with them.

1559
00:51:35,525 --> 00:51:36,584
From the granny's place we just saw?

1560
00:51:36,585 --> 00:51:38,925
- How long has he been on about it?
- Let's get a box each.

1561
00:51:39,095 --> 00:51:40,894
- In the middle...
- If he doesn't get one today,

1562
00:51:40,895 --> 00:51:41,895
he's going to cry.

1563
00:51:42,025 --> 00:51:43,965
We have to go to the shop for him.

1564
00:51:44,065 --> 00:51:47,465
You have to get some walnut cakes when you're in Cheonan.

1565
00:51:47,765 --> 00:51:50,105
When I came here to film last time,

1566
00:51:50,405 --> 00:51:51,535
I left without buying them.

1567
00:51:52,275 --> 00:51:54,375
- Come on. We have to try them.
- I just left.

1568
00:51:55,875 --> 00:51:57,045
- Hey, Jae Myung.
- Yes.

1569
00:51:58,005 --> 00:52:00,515
It's awkward to see you speak casually to Jae Myung.

1570
00:52:01,915 --> 00:52:04,145
- You'll get used to it.
- Of course.

1571
00:52:04,585 --> 00:52:06,614
I think it's because you didn't dye your hair black.

1572
00:52:06,615 --> 00:52:08,054
- Right. It's for work.
- Right.

1573
00:52:08,055 --> 00:52:09,884
I do look younger when I dye my hair black.

1574
00:52:09,885 --> 00:52:12,494
- Actually...
- Once I shave and all.

1575
00:52:12,495 --> 00:52:15,224
Actually, he's young,

1576
00:52:15,225 --> 00:52:16,525
- but the roles he has played...
- Yes.

1577
00:52:16,825 --> 00:52:19,935
- include a corporate mogul.
- Yes.

1578
00:52:20,235 --> 00:52:21,434
In the military setting,

1579
00:52:21,435 --> 00:52:23,064
- he'd be a two-star general.
- That's right.

1580
00:52:23,065 --> 00:52:24,534
- He'd be at least a two-star.
- Yes.

1581
00:52:24,535 --> 00:52:26,005
- The NIS director.
- The director of the NIS.

1582
00:52:26,505 --> 00:52:28,805
- And professors.
- He plays high-ranking positions.

1583
00:52:29,445 --> 00:52:30,974
Honestly, I look young, don't I?

1584
00:52:30,975 --> 00:52:32,074
- You do.
- Right?

1585
00:52:32,075 --> 00:52:34,374
- Compared to the roles you play.
- Exactly.

1586
00:52:34,375 --> 00:52:35,645
Some think I'm 60 years old.

1587
00:52:38,455 --> 00:52:39,755
Did you start out in theater?

1588
00:52:39,915 --> 00:52:42,725
I did theater in college. I was in the theater club.

1589
00:52:43,485 --> 00:52:44,995
I was based only in Busan.

1590
00:52:45,325 --> 00:52:47,425
And it's been 13 years since I started working in Seoul.

1591
00:52:47,955 --> 00:52:50,925
- It hasn't been long.
- No. I came when I was over 40.

1592
00:52:51,295 --> 00:52:54,795
(He left the Busan theater scene he was part of for 15 years...)

1593
00:52:55,305 --> 00:52:59,275
(and started from the bottom by taking numerous auditions.)

1594
00:53:00,305 --> 00:53:02,245
(Producer: Let's do this together.)

1595
00:53:02,945 --> 00:53:06,775
(Finally, the public started to respond to his performance.)

1596
00:53:08,785 --> 00:53:11,885
(He has established himself with his distinctive acting.)

1597
00:53:12,015 --> 00:53:13,714
(Yoo Jae Myung, the actor you can always count on)

1598
00:53:13,715 --> 00:53:15,184
I see. You used to...

1599
00:53:15,185 --> 00:53:16,685
- work only in Busan.
- Yes.

1600
00:53:17,655 --> 00:53:19,194
- Hey, it's here.
- It's here.

1601
00:53:19,195 --> 00:53:21,095
- Gosh. It smells amazing.
- Gosh. It looks good.

1602
00:53:21,455 --> 00:53:23,494
- This is...
- The sesame oil smells incredible.

1603
00:53:23,495 --> 00:53:24,765
This sesame oil is...

1604
00:53:25,135 --> 00:53:27,065
I've never tried this combination before.

1605
00:53:27,195 --> 00:53:29,204
- It has to be good.
- That's right.

1606
00:53:29,205 --> 00:53:30,534
- This combination.
- That's true.

1607
00:53:30,535 --> 00:53:31,605
This sauce.

1608
00:53:32,005 --> 00:53:34,175
It smells incredible.

1609
00:53:35,075 --> 00:53:37,675
(Slurping right away)

1610
00:53:40,975 --> 00:53:41,984
(Exclaiming)

1611
00:53:41,985 --> 00:53:43,185
- It's good, isn't it?
- Yes.

1612
00:53:44,285 --> 00:53:45,355
It's delicious.

1613
00:53:47,315 --> 00:53:48,685
- Is this perilla oil?
- Yes.

1614
00:53:49,285 --> 00:53:50,625
- Soy sauce and perilla oil.
- It's... Yes.

1615
00:53:52,125 --> 00:53:53,395
It's seasoned.

1616
00:53:53,525 --> 00:53:55,164
- It's a delicacy.
- It's good.

1617
00:53:55,165 --> 00:53:56,225
It tastes good.

1618
00:53:57,695 --> 00:53:59,565
(The noodle aficionado is satisfied too.)

1619
00:53:59,695 --> 00:54:01,465
You're a cool type.

1620
00:54:01,865 --> 00:54:03,004
You finished it already.

1621
00:54:03,005 --> 00:54:04,235
(A happy meal in Cheonan)

1622
00:54:06,135 --> 00:54:07,235
Goodness.

1623
00:54:08,105 --> 00:54:09,304
- As for the game, - Yes.

1624
00:54:09,305 --> 00:54:10,305
Should we play the bomb game?

1625
00:54:10,775 --> 00:54:11,875
What's that?

1626
00:54:12,175 --> 00:54:13,415
We'll set a timer.

1627
00:54:13,745 --> 00:54:15,815
We'll play the Word Chain while passing the phone around.

1628
00:54:16,015 --> 00:54:17,915
When the alarm goes off, that person has to pay.

1629
00:54:18,085 --> 00:54:20,015
- We don't know the set time.
- No, we don't.

1630
00:54:20,155 --> 00:54:22,154
You can go first, Jae Myung. Let's go this way.

1631
00:54:22,155 --> 00:54:23,685
- Okay.
- Okay. Good.

1632
00:54:24,285 --> 00:54:25,824
- I set the timer.
- Do we start right away?

1633
00:54:25,825 --> 00:54:26,854
- Yes.
- Yes.

1634
00:54:26,855 --> 00:54:28,024
- It's started.
- The Word Chain?

1635
00:54:28,025 --> 00:54:29,325
Yes. You can start now.

1636
00:54:30,195 --> 00:54:31,265
Makguksu.

1637
00:54:31,695 --> 00:54:32,935
- I think...
- Wednesday.

1638
00:54:33,165 --> 00:54:34,295
- Wednesday?
- Did you push the button?

1639
00:54:34,565 --> 00:54:35,502
Handcart.

1640
00:54:35,865 --> 00:54:36,964
- Garage.
- Gosh.

1641
00:54:36,965 --> 00:54:37,705
Tire.

1642
00:54:38,675 --> 00:54:40,534
- Fishcake.
- It's getting close.

1643
00:54:40,535 --> 00:54:41,674
- It might go off.
- Mukho.

1644
00:54:41,675 --> 00:54:43,244
- What?
- Mukho.

1645
00:54:43,245 --> 00:54:44,575
- What?
- Tiger.

1646
00:54:44,945 --> 00:54:46,245
- Yes!
- What?

1647
00:54:46,575 --> 00:54:48,274
- Tiger.
- Yes!

1648
00:54:48,275 --> 00:54:49,414
It ended with him.

1649
00:54:49,415 --> 00:54:50,785
(The alarm went off as soon as he passed the phone.)

1650
00:54:51,385 --> 00:54:52,885
I had a hunch.

1651
00:54:53,455 --> 00:54:55,354
(Paying for lunch and walnut cakes at once)

1652
00:54:55,355 --> 00:54:57,524
- It's 30 walnut cakes.
- You're amazing, Jae Seok!

1653
00:54:57,525 --> 00:54:58,624
It will be 30 walnut cakes.

1654
00:54:58,625 --> 00:55:00,194
Jae Seok. I'll tell Mom...

1655
00:55:00,195 --> 00:55:01,894
you bought the walnut cakes for her.

1656
00:55:01,895 --> 00:55:02,994
You'll buy 30 walnut cakes.

1657
00:55:02,995 --> 00:55:05,265
"Honey, this is from Jae Seok."

1658
00:55:06,065 --> 00:55:07,635
(Laughing)

1659
00:55:07,765 --> 00:55:09,135
- We'll enjoy them.
- Good.

1660
00:55:10,165 --> 00:55:12,434
- That was close. I had a hunch.
- We'll enjoy them.

1661
00:55:12,435 --> 00:55:13,875
That's why he reached out his hand.

1662
00:55:14,205 --> 00:55:15,905
If I had passed it, it would've been Yeon Seok.

1663
00:55:17,905 --> 00:55:19,474
- Shall we?
- Yes.

1664
00:55:19,475 --> 00:55:21,474
Thanks for the food, Jae Seok.

1665
00:55:21,475 --> 00:55:22,814
(Paying with Jae Seok Pay)

1666
00:55:22,815 --> 00:55:25,045
- You're amazing.
- I'm disappointed.

1667
00:55:25,315 --> 00:55:26,914
- Thank you.
- Thanks.

1668
00:55:26,915 --> 00:55:28,154
- Have a good day.
- Bye.

1669
00:55:28,155 --> 00:55:29,325
Hope you do well.

1670
00:55:29,655 --> 00:55:31,925
Gosh. We should go get walnut cakes.

1671
00:55:32,925 --> 00:55:34,054
Gosh. Those walnut cakes.

1672
00:55:34,055 --> 00:55:36,195
Walnut cakes Jae Seok bought.

1673
00:55:36,295 --> 00:55:37,365
Yes.

1674
00:55:37,865 --> 00:55:40,294
I play Word Chain three times a day...

1675
00:55:40,295 --> 00:55:41,864
- because of my kid.
- Really? With your son. Right.

1676
00:55:41,865 --> 00:55:43,134
Yes. Because of my son.

1677
00:55:43,135 --> 00:55:44,735
I felt comfortable playing Word Chain.

1678
00:55:45,775 --> 00:55:47,535
- We should've played a harder one.
- I know.

1679
00:55:48,475 --> 00:55:50,145
- How old is he?
- He's eight.

1680
00:55:50,505 --> 00:55:52,715
- He's already 8.
- He's 8.

1681
00:55:53,445 --> 00:55:55,684
- When my son watches this, - Yes.

1682
00:55:55,685 --> 00:55:58,585
- he'll find it super hilarious.
- He'll love it.

1683
00:55:58,685 --> 00:56:00,214
- He'll ask to play the game.
- That's right.

1684
00:56:00,215 --> 00:56:01,615
(Smiling just thinking about his son)

1685
00:56:01,885 --> 00:56:03,524
I'm walking on air.

1686
00:56:03,525 --> 00:56:04,585
- Right?
- It does feel different.

1687
00:56:04,725 --> 00:56:07,094
Yeon Seok loves it when I have to pay.

1688
00:56:07,095 --> 00:56:08,194
We're in Cheonan.

1689
00:56:08,195 --> 00:56:09,795
I love it when he has to pay too.

1690
00:56:09,925 --> 00:56:12,334
- Yeon Seok.
- Should we get some coffee to go?

1691
00:56:12,335 --> 00:56:14,265
- Sounds good. Shall we do that?
- Coffee isn't included.

1692
00:56:15,095 --> 00:56:16,404
- Let's play another game.
- Coffee isn't included.

1693
00:56:16,405 --> 00:56:18,065
Let's play another game and take coffee to go.

1694
00:56:18,335 --> 00:56:19,834
- For coffee...
- We have to play another game.

1695
00:56:19,835 --> 00:56:21,034
- Let's play another one.
- Are we playing a game again?

1696
00:56:21,035 --> 00:56:22,305
Why don't we simply play rock-paper-scissors?

1697
00:56:22,505 --> 00:56:23,544
- Let's do that.
- It's simple.

1698
00:56:23,545 --> 00:56:24,675
- Rock-paper-scissors?
- It's simple.

1699
00:56:25,005 --> 00:56:26,944
No throw, you lose. Rock-paper-scissors.

1700
00:56:26,945 --> 00:56:28,645
(Scissors, scissors, scissors, rock)

1701
00:56:28,815 --> 00:56:30,915
Yes. The youngest always wins.

1702
00:56:31,985 --> 00:56:33,985
Sun Bin is in her hometown,

1703
00:56:34,155 --> 00:56:36,224
- and she's feeling great.
- She has Cheonan's energy.

1704
00:56:36,225 --> 00:56:38,825
- Rock-paper-scissors.
- Rock-paper-scissors.

1705
00:56:39,155 --> 00:56:40,454
- You were slow.
- I threw rock.

1706
00:56:40,455 --> 00:56:41,925
- You didn't throw anything.
- I threw rock.

1707
00:56:42,025 --> 00:56:43,464
- You didn't throw.
- I threw rock.

1708
00:56:43,465 --> 00:56:45,594
- You didn't.
- It was rock, Jae Myung.

1709
00:56:45,595 --> 00:56:46,764
If you didn't throw, it's over.

1710
00:56:46,765 --> 00:56:48,464
- No throw, you lose.
- Right.

1711
00:56:48,465 --> 00:56:49,904
- I said, "No throw, you lose."
- I had it here.

1712
00:56:49,905 --> 00:56:51,434
- I had it here.
- I said, "No throw, you lose."

1713
00:56:51,435 --> 00:56:52,805
Let's cut him some slack since he paid for lunch.

1714
00:56:53,775 --> 00:56:55,375
No throw, you lose. Rock-paper-scissors.

1715
00:56:55,505 --> 00:56:56,805
Gosh. That was close.

1716
00:56:57,075 --> 00:56:58,204
Rock-paper-scissors.

1717
00:56:58,205 --> 00:56:59,445
(Scissors, scissors, rock)

1718
00:56:59,615 --> 00:57:01,345
- Yes!
- It's...

1719
00:57:01,515 --> 00:57:02,614
- Jae Myung.
- Jae Seok.

1720
00:57:02,615 --> 00:57:04,445
Yeon Seok should pay.

1721
00:57:04,745 --> 00:57:06,255
- Yes.
- It's driving me crazy.

1722
00:57:06,485 --> 00:57:07,684
What's with you?

1723
00:57:07,685 --> 00:57:09,184
- Okay. Rock-paper-scissors.
- Rock-paper-scissors.

1724
00:57:09,185 --> 00:57:11,225
(Win, lose)

1725
00:57:12,555 --> 00:57:15,195
- The older ones are buying today.
- Good.

1726
00:57:15,495 --> 00:57:16,824
- I'll buy the cold drinks.
- Great.

1727
00:57:16,825 --> 00:57:17,925
Others should get one too.

1728
00:57:18,635 --> 00:57:20,335
I'll get an iced Americano.

1729
00:57:20,795 --> 00:57:22,435
An iced Americano.

1730
00:57:23,135 --> 00:57:25,635
Do you prefer less ice?

1731
00:57:25,905 --> 00:57:27,875
Less... No, more ice will be fine.

1732
00:57:28,235 --> 00:57:29,974
- More ice. Just order it like that.
- I'll order it like this. Okay?

1733
00:57:29,975 --> 00:57:31,374
- Is it seven dollars?
- Pay.

1734
00:57:31,375 --> 00:57:33,775
- Is it only 7 dollars for 3 drinks?
- Pay.

1735
00:57:36,685 --> 00:57:39,055
(Feeling super happy)

1736
00:57:40,185 --> 00:57:41,555
I can't help but hum.

1737
00:57:46,495 --> 00:57:47,525
You feel great today.

1738
00:57:48,425 --> 00:57:49,464
I do. It's because you're paying.

1739
00:57:49,465 --> 00:57:50,895
You'll get ten walnut cakes.

1740
00:57:51,495 --> 00:57:52,964
Come on. Why?

1741
00:57:52,965 --> 00:57:54,134
- Because he's annoying?
- Yes. You'll get ten...

1742
00:57:54,135 --> 00:57:55,304
because you're annoying.

1743
00:57:55,305 --> 00:57:58,004
- Why?
- I can't stand you. You'll get ten.

1744
00:57:58,005 --> 00:57:59,104
Why?

1745
00:57:59,105 --> 00:58:01,034
- Enough is enough. You'll get ten.
- You can't do that.

1746
00:58:01,035 --> 00:58:02,605
- You can't do that.
- Yeon Seok gets ten walnut cakes.

1747
00:58:03,345 --> 00:58:05,074
- Jae Myung will get 30.
- Yes.

1748
00:58:05,075 --> 00:58:06,145
(Polite)

1749
00:58:06,375 --> 00:58:07,575
- He gets 30.
- He has a son.

1750
00:58:07,875 --> 00:58:09,744
- He has a kid.
- Don't discriminate.

1751
00:58:09,745 --> 00:58:10,914
- I don't discriminate.
- I need to...

1752
00:58:10,915 --> 00:58:12,184
I need to share them with my neighbors.

1753
00:58:12,185 --> 00:58:13,815
- And tell them he bought them.
- Yes. He has a kid.

1754
00:58:14,385 --> 00:58:15,484
(The drinks are ready already.)

1755
00:58:15,485 --> 00:58:18,025
Thank you. Have a good day.

1756
00:58:18,655 --> 00:58:21,954
By the way, they put so much ice.

1757
00:58:21,955 --> 00:58:23,794
They were serious when they said "more ice."

1758
00:58:23,795 --> 00:58:24,965
(More ice)

1759
00:58:26,035 --> 00:58:27,864
- Cheonan is...
- There's no room for the straw.

1760
00:58:27,865 --> 00:58:29,364
Cheonan people are so generous.

1761
00:58:29,365 --> 00:58:30,434
- Yes.
- Drink it as is.

1762
00:58:30,435 --> 00:58:32,804
Why did you choose "more ice?"

1763
00:58:32,805 --> 00:58:34,174
- It's...
- The ice is...

1764
00:58:34,175 --> 00:58:36,345
We don't need this many ice cubes.

1765
00:58:36,845 --> 00:58:38,674
- It's almost as if...
- This is...

1766
00:58:38,675 --> 00:58:40,445
How did it happen? Let's go now.

1767
00:58:41,045 --> 00:58:42,414
- The people in Cheonan...
- We can enjoy it cold.

1768
00:58:42,415 --> 00:58:44,415
- They're generous with their ice.
- Yes.

1769
00:58:44,585 --> 00:58:45,954
Let's run. Run.

1770
00:58:45,955 --> 00:58:47,054
Are we crossing at this light?

1771
00:58:47,055 --> 00:58:48,254
- Run.
- No.

1772
00:58:48,255 --> 00:58:50,084
Why is he so fast?

1773
00:58:50,085 --> 00:58:51,125
Run.

1774
00:58:51,625 --> 00:58:52,725
Hello.

1775
00:58:52,925 --> 00:58:54,125
- Are these free samples?
- Yes.

1776
00:58:54,555 --> 00:58:56,125
- An assorted...
- The medium has 30 walnut cakes.

1777
00:58:56,295 --> 00:58:57,464
Yes, you're right.

1778
00:58:57,465 --> 00:58:59,765
They cook them right in there.

1779
00:59:00,235 --> 00:59:01,864
Look at them.

1780
00:59:01,865 --> 00:59:03,065
- They're making them now.
- Then each cake costs...

1781
00:59:03,365 --> 00:59:05,734
- Okay.
- It's a total of eight boxes.

1782
00:59:05,735 --> 00:59:07,505
- It's 56 dollars.
- Okay.

1783
00:59:07,905 --> 00:59:08,905
Thank you, Jae Seok.

1784
00:59:08,906 --> 00:59:10,804
- No.
- Thank you.

1785
00:59:10,805 --> 00:59:12,244
- Pardon?
- Try this.

1786
00:59:12,245 --> 00:59:13,414
- Are they samples to try?
- Is this it?

1787
00:59:13,415 --> 00:59:14,814
- What is it?
- Yes, it is.

1788
00:59:14,815 --> 00:59:16,544
- They are free samples.
- Free samples? Oh, right.

1789
00:59:16,545 --> 00:59:17,845
They're fresh out of the oven.

1790
00:59:18,345 --> 00:59:19,484
Gosh. Then I need to try one.

1791
00:59:19,485 --> 00:59:20,614
(Putting it straight into his mouth)

1792
00:59:20,615 --> 00:59:24,185
I'll get 2 white bean paste ones and 2 red bean paste ones.

1793
00:59:24,255 --> 00:59:26,254
- Gosh.
- I'll pay separately.

1794
00:59:26,255 --> 00:59:28,355
- It's good.
- It's tasty.

1795
00:59:28,425 --> 00:59:30,295
- Gosh. The bean paste is good.
- Yes. It's delicious.

1796
00:59:30,965 --> 00:59:32,695
- A total of four boxes?
- Yes.

1797
00:59:35,605 --> 00:59:37,235
The bean paste tastes incredible.

1798
00:59:37,405 --> 00:59:39,135
Cheonan is all about walnut cakes.

1799
00:59:40,905 --> 00:59:42,244
- Sun Bin.
- Gosh.

1800
00:59:42,245 --> 00:59:43,274
Hello.

1801
00:59:43,275 --> 00:59:45,575
- Yes, thank you.
- Yes.

1802
00:59:46,415 --> 00:59:48,075
It's delicious. Awesome.

1803
00:59:48,275 --> 00:59:49,484
- It's good. It's warm.
- It tastes good.

1804
00:59:49,485 --> 00:59:50,985
It's very rich.

1805
00:59:51,045 --> 00:59:54,115
I'll get 2 white bean paste ones and 2 red bean paste ones.

1806
00:59:54,215 --> 00:59:55,554
I'll pay separately.

1807
00:59:55,555 --> 00:59:56,825
- It's good.
- It's tasty.

1808
00:59:57,185 --> 00:59:58,185
Gosh, Jae Seok.

1809
00:59:58,186 --> 00:59:59,725
There's a reason why it's good and famous.

1810
01:00:00,395 --> 01:00:02,765
- If he doesn't get walnut cakes...
- Gosh. I love it.

1811
01:00:02,925 --> 01:00:05,094
Cheonan is famous for its walnut cakes for a reason.

1812
01:00:05,095 --> 01:00:06,295
You got your wish.

1813
01:00:06,795 --> 01:00:08,265
Is the place near here?

1814
01:00:08,295 --> 01:00:10,005
We'll make a turn here.

1815
01:00:11,605 --> 01:00:12,835
It's from Lee Won Ho.

1816
01:00:13,435 --> 01:00:15,674
"As both family and colleagues,"

1817
01:00:15,675 --> 01:00:18,505
"we're creating flavors shaped by years of tradition."

1818
01:00:19,215 --> 01:00:21,945
"For my hero who has raised me,"

1819
01:00:22,185 --> 01:00:23,884
"please present her with a gift..."

1820
01:00:23,885 --> 01:00:25,584
"that could be remembered after 100 years."

1821
01:00:25,585 --> 01:00:27,355
- "My hero?"
- "My hero?"

1822
01:00:28,255 --> 01:00:30,154
- The family must be doing...
- Right.

1823
01:00:30,155 --> 01:00:31,555
- Do they cook together?
- Do they work together?

1824
01:00:32,455 --> 01:00:33,894
They create flavors.

1825
01:00:33,895 --> 01:00:35,325
They must be running a restaurant.

1826
01:00:35,665 --> 01:00:36,994
This is it. On the second floor.

1827
01:00:36,995 --> 01:00:37,995
(Does the second Whenever Guest create something together?)

1828
01:00:38,795 --> 01:00:40,564
We were right about this.

1829
01:00:40,565 --> 01:00:41,565
We were right.

1830
01:00:41,795 --> 01:00:43,834
- That's it. Over there.
- They sell dumpling stew.

1831
01:00:43,835 --> 01:00:45,805
I craved dumplings so much today.

1832
01:00:47,705 --> 01:00:50,645
(They go upstairs following the smell of dumplings.)

1833
01:00:50,675 --> 01:00:52,415
Is this it? Hello?

1834
01:00:52,715 --> 01:00:54,074
Did you send a letter to us?

1835
01:00:54,075 --> 01:00:55,244
- Yes.
- Lee Won Ho.

1836
01:00:55,245 --> 01:00:56,915
- Hello.
- Hi.

1837
01:00:57,015 --> 01:00:58,144
- Nice to meet you.
- Nice to meet you.

1838
01:00:58,145 --> 01:00:59,555
- Hello.
- Nice to meet you.

1839
01:00:59,785 --> 01:01:00,954
You've come a long way. Sorry.

1840
01:01:00,955 --> 01:01:03,284
- We should've eaten lunch here.
- Welcome. Yes.

1841
01:01:03,285 --> 01:01:04,655
(Lee Won Ho, the second Whenever Guest)

1842
01:01:05,185 --> 01:01:06,255
Nice to meet you.

1843
01:01:06,695 --> 01:01:08,725
We didn't know it was a dumpling stew place.

1844
01:01:09,595 --> 01:01:10,924
(But where are the rest of his family?)

1845
01:01:10,925 --> 01:01:12,265
- Where do we go?
- Where should we sit?

1846
01:01:12,535 --> 01:01:14,534
The lunch hours are almost over.

1847
01:01:14,535 --> 01:01:16,004
- I see.
- Mother is resting upstairs.

1848
01:01:16,005 --> 01:01:17,534
- You can join her.
- She's upstairs.

1849
01:01:17,535 --> 01:01:18,974
- I'll take you upstairs. Let's go.
- Okay.

1850
01:01:18,975 --> 01:01:20,404
We can take the elevator.

1851
01:01:20,405 --> 01:01:21,735
- Go up one more floor?
- Yes.

1852
01:01:21,875 --> 01:01:24,005
Our house is on the fourth floor.

1853
01:01:24,475 --> 01:01:26,015
- You can go up there now.
- I see.

1854
01:01:27,875 --> 01:01:29,814
- Just with dumplings.
- They sell dumplings.

1855
01:01:29,815 --> 01:01:31,845
- They only sell dumplings.
- I really wanted to have them.

1856
01:01:32,715 --> 01:01:34,015
(The restaurant is on the 2nd floor.)

1857
01:01:34,955 --> 01:01:36,484
My mother lives on the fourth floor.

1858
01:01:36,485 --> 01:01:38,784
I live with my family on the fifth floor.

1859
01:01:38,785 --> 01:01:39,895
We live with my mother.

1860
01:01:40,095 --> 01:01:41,124
The three generations live together.

1861
01:01:41,125 --> 01:01:44,164
Literally, you live close to work with your family.

1862
01:01:44,165 --> 01:01:45,595
- Yes, you're right.
- Right.

1863
01:01:45,995 --> 01:01:47,235
That's awesome.

1864
01:01:48,735 --> 01:01:50,664
- This is our house.
- So this is your house.

1865
01:01:50,665 --> 01:01:52,474
- Yes.
- Hello.

1866
01:01:52,475 --> 01:01:53,935
- Please come in.
- Hello.

1867
01:01:54,205 --> 01:01:55,374
- Gosh.
- Is this the living room?

1868
01:01:55,375 --> 01:01:56,475
Gosh. Hello.

1869
01:01:57,005 --> 01:01:58,644
Gosh. You run a restaurant...

1870
01:01:58,645 --> 01:01:59,874
while living upstairs over here.

1871
01:01:59,875 --> 01:02:01,074
- Yes.
- Nice to meet you.

1872
01:02:01,075 --> 01:02:02,544
This is your real house.

1873
01:02:02,545 --> 01:02:03,845
Yes. It's a house.

1874
01:02:04,385 --> 01:02:05,415
It's...

1875
01:02:05,655 --> 01:02:07,254
Gosh. You've come all the way here. My goodness.

1876
01:02:07,255 --> 01:02:08,455
It's nothing. Thank you.

1877
01:02:08,815 --> 01:02:11,454
If we had known this was a dumpling stew place...

1878
01:02:11,455 --> 01:02:12,524
We would've come here.

1879
01:02:12,525 --> 01:02:14,755
I felt like eating dumplings today.

1880
01:02:15,795 --> 01:02:17,824
I took over her restaurant.

1881
01:02:17,825 --> 01:02:19,494
- We sell North Korean dumplings.
- Right.

1882
01:02:19,495 --> 01:02:21,294
My mother ran the restaurant for 27 years.

1883
01:02:21,295 --> 01:02:22,905
I'm her son, and I inherited it.

1884
01:02:23,205 --> 01:02:25,505
- It's been six years.
- Nice.

1885
01:02:25,805 --> 01:02:27,974
There aren't many places like this.

1886
01:02:27,975 --> 01:02:29,475
- No, there aren't.
- No.

1887
01:02:29,805 --> 01:02:31,274
- I did a lot of...
- I thought...

1888
01:02:31,275 --> 01:02:32,414
Are those dumplings?

1889
01:02:32,415 --> 01:02:33,975
She's preparing dumplings for you.

1890
01:02:34,475 --> 01:02:35,885
- Really?
- We should...

1891
01:02:36,185 --> 01:02:38,184
- try them at least.
- You should have some dumplings.

1892
01:02:38,185 --> 01:02:40,185
Gosh. They're dumplings with 40 years of history.

1893
01:02:40,355 --> 01:02:41,355
- Thank you.
- Yes.

1894
01:02:41,356 --> 01:02:43,325
- The table is too small.
- Have some dumplings.

1895
01:02:44,985 --> 01:02:46,094
They already smell good.

1896
01:02:46,095 --> 01:02:47,095
(Warm)

1897
01:02:47,295 --> 01:02:48,555
Thank you.

1898
01:02:50,665 --> 01:02:52,895
It smells different. My goodness.

1899
01:02:53,865 --> 01:02:55,905
(Satisfied)

1900
01:02:58,765 --> 01:03:00,505
Our dumplings are known...

1901
01:03:00,635 --> 01:03:02,444
- for their light taste.
- It's tasty.

1902
01:03:02,445 --> 01:03:04,105
We chop and prepare the filling...

1903
01:03:04,705 --> 01:03:06,274
- from scratch.
- Gosh.

1904
01:03:06,275 --> 01:03:07,715
- Yes.
- Then it has to be good.

1905
01:03:07,815 --> 01:03:09,414
- The sauce is delicious too.
- Yes.

1906
01:03:09,415 --> 01:03:12,985
Even when a family makes dumplings for a living,

1907
01:03:13,515 --> 01:03:15,824
it's not that common...

1908
01:03:15,825 --> 01:03:18,124
for their children to inherit it...

1909
01:03:18,125 --> 01:03:20,455
and continue the tradition these days.

1910
01:03:20,625 --> 01:03:23,864
I thought of carrying on her business...

1911
01:03:23,865 --> 01:03:26,395
since I was young.

1912
01:03:26,795 --> 01:03:29,805
She got ill all of a sudden.

1913
01:03:30,005 --> 01:03:32,675
She battled colon cancer for a while.

1914
01:03:33,075 --> 01:03:35,275
She couldn't run the restaurant anymore.

1915
01:03:35,605 --> 01:03:37,144
We couldn't just close it...

1916
01:03:37,145 --> 01:03:39,474
- after running it for 27 years.
- That's true.

1917
01:03:39,475 --> 01:03:42,384
There were many regulars too.

1918
01:03:42,385 --> 01:03:43,645
So we couldn't close the restaurant.

1919
01:03:43,945 --> 01:03:46,655
I had worked as a chef for a long time too.

1920
01:03:46,955 --> 01:03:49,685
- Yes. I worked in Seoul.
- In what area of cooking?

1921
01:03:49,825 --> 01:03:53,025
I worked at Hotel S for 18 years.

1922
01:03:53,625 --> 01:03:55,154
- I cooked French cuisine.
- You cooked French cuisine.

1923
01:03:55,155 --> 01:03:58,024
- There's a French restaurant there.
- Yes.

1924
01:03:58,025 --> 01:04:01,364
I worked there for about 18 years.

1925
01:04:01,365 --> 01:04:03,464
(He received a presidential honor for his outstanding cooking skills.)

1926
01:04:03,465 --> 01:04:05,605
When I got the medal, I went to the Blue House.

1927
01:04:05,705 --> 01:04:08,234
They sent us a photo of me shaking hands with the president.

1928
01:04:08,235 --> 01:04:10,275
- When you got awarded.
- Yes.

1929
01:04:10,605 --> 01:04:13,115
- That's family treasure.
- Yes.

1930
01:04:13,175 --> 01:04:16,014
Since then, he had cooked in the Blue House.

1931
01:04:16,015 --> 01:04:18,444
- Nice.
- I didn't do it alone.

1932
01:04:18,445 --> 01:04:20,555
A team from Hotel S went there.

1933
01:04:20,815 --> 01:04:22,885
We went there every time the president had an event.

1934
01:04:23,225 --> 01:04:25,055
I met with all presidents.

1935
01:04:25,355 --> 01:04:26,724
Gosh. I see.

1936
01:04:26,725 --> 01:04:29,124
So I was working fine,

1937
01:04:29,125 --> 01:04:30,964
but since Mother got sick,

1938
01:04:30,965 --> 01:04:33,035
I thought I needed to live with her.

1939
01:04:33,265 --> 01:04:35,865
That was when I decided to do so.

1940
01:04:36,135 --> 01:04:38,135
Gosh. In this day and age,

1941
01:04:38,265 --> 01:04:40,174
that's not a matter of course.

1942
01:04:40,175 --> 01:04:41,975
You had your own job before.

1943
01:04:42,105 --> 01:04:44,645
How did you feel when he said he'd take over your restaurant?

1944
01:04:45,075 --> 01:04:46,575
I was a bit sad.

1945
01:04:47,645 --> 01:04:49,685
He studied a lot and had much experience.

1946
01:04:50,285 --> 01:04:53,384
He competed in WorldSkills,

1947
01:04:53,385 --> 01:04:56,224
and he was the first in Asia to win a medal.

1948
01:04:56,225 --> 01:04:57,225
(Understanding)

1949
01:04:57,355 --> 01:04:58,955
(Smiling)

1950
01:04:59,295 --> 01:05:01,124
He got a medal from the president.

1951
01:05:01,125 --> 01:05:03,595
Like I always say, parents tend...

1952
01:05:04,195 --> 01:05:05,965
to cherish the titles of their children.

1953
01:05:06,735 --> 01:05:08,235
They mention them so smoothly.

1954
01:05:08,595 --> 01:05:10,664
- It was as expected.
- It was expected.

1955
01:05:10,665 --> 01:05:13,134
So I was a little sad.

1956
01:05:13,135 --> 01:05:16,005
But he wanted to come and live with me.

1957
01:05:16,105 --> 01:05:17,444
So I said, "Okay. Let's live together."

1958
01:05:17,445 --> 01:05:18,805
And here we are now.

1959
01:05:19,315 --> 01:05:20,514
- Also, thankfully, - And...

1960
01:05:20,515 --> 01:05:22,145
he got the presidential medal.

1961
01:05:23,545 --> 01:05:26,184
- I told you.
- That was smooth.

1962
01:05:26,185 --> 01:05:27,384
That was very smooth.

1963
01:05:27,385 --> 01:05:29,085
She can't help mentioning it.

1964
01:05:29,385 --> 01:05:30,924
I just love it.

1965
01:05:30,925 --> 01:05:32,924
You love that the president awarded him.

1966
01:05:32,925 --> 01:05:34,154
- You love that the most.
- I do.

1967
01:05:34,155 --> 01:05:35,524
- He got awarded by the president.
- I'm proud of him.

1968
01:05:35,525 --> 01:05:37,065
I see. Every parent is the same.

1969
01:05:37,165 --> 01:05:38,264
Honestly,

1970
01:05:38,265 --> 01:05:39,864
- I heard it's really tough...
- Yes.

1971
01:05:39,865 --> 01:05:41,794
When you do business with your family.

1972
01:05:41,795 --> 01:05:43,504
- That's right.
- We argue all the time.

1973
01:05:43,505 --> 01:05:44,605
- Exactly.
- Yes.

1974
01:05:44,865 --> 01:05:46,305
We have disagreements every day.

1975
01:05:47,135 --> 01:05:48,574
In your mother's eyes,

1976
01:05:48,575 --> 01:05:50,374
there must be things she's not satisfied with.

1977
01:05:50,375 --> 01:05:52,074
You're right. She yelled at me all the time.

1978
01:05:52,075 --> 01:05:53,244
She isn't satisfied even if you tried so hard.

1979
01:05:53,245 --> 01:05:55,245
- I scolded him every day.
- Right.

1980
01:05:55,375 --> 01:05:57,785
You look like part of the family when you're sitting like that.

1981
01:05:58,615 --> 01:06:00,254
I have a bad knee right now.

1982
01:06:00,255 --> 01:06:01,454
- Exactly.
- I sat like a mother.

1983
01:06:01,455 --> 01:06:03,124
I thought he was part of the family.

1984
01:06:03,125 --> 01:06:05,655
- The three of them are a family.
